sentences.l:
but at the tip
she bound a golden yoke, fair, and on it the yoke-straps
fine she laid, golden;

But Athena, daughter of aegis-bearing Zeus,
poured down the robe, fine, on her father’s floor,
intricate, which she herself made and toiled at with her hands;

there, having stood, she shouted, the goddess, white-armed Hera,
likening herself to great-spirited Stentor, bronze-voiced,
who called out as loud as fifty others;

As long as heavenly Achilles used to go into the war,
never did the Trojans before the Dardanian gates
use to go about;

And even when I would not let him fight
nor dash out, when he came away from the Achaeans
as a messenger to Thebes among the many Cadmeans;

but he, keeping his own strong spirit, as before,
kept challenging the young men of the Cadmeans, and won at everything
easily;
